insert into public.court_verdict_templates (
  case_id,outcome_key,headline,what_court_heard,verdict,court_ordered_move,share_text,tone_condition
) values
('CASE-046','spectrum_1','TRANSPARENCY + TRANSPARENCY','You both want full visibility into each other''s finances — no separate accounts, no unexplained purchases.','You''re aligned here, which is genuinely low-risk. The only thing to watch is making sure "full visibility" doesn''t turn into monitoring each other''s every purchase.','[username A] sentenced to: do one no-agenda money check-in this week, just to keep it collaborative rather than surveillance-y. [username B] sentenced to: same — take turns leading it. Set a recurring monthly "money date" — 15 minutes, no judgment, just visibility.','Bunny Court closed Financial Transparency Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-046','spectrum_2','TRANSPARENCY + BALANCED','One of you wants full financial visibility. The other''s comfortable with some shared, some separate.','Not really a conflict — the balanced partner has room to share a bit more without it costing their sense of independence.','[Transparency-leaning username] sentenced to: name specifically what visibility would actually feel reassuring, rather than "everything." [Balanced username] sentenced to: share one thing you''d normally keep separate, just to test the waters. Pick one shared financial goal to track together this month, regardless of how the rest stays split.','Bunny Court closed Financial Transparency Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-046','spectrum_3','TRANSPARENCY + PRIVACY','One of you wants full financial visibility into everything. The other values keeping some financial independence, even in a serious relationship.','Neither approach is a red flag on its own — one of you finds security in full visibility, the other finds it in autonomy. The friction is really about what "trust" looks like, not about hiding anything.','[Transparency-leaning username] sentenced to: name the actual worry behind wanting full access, rather than just asking for it. [Privacy-leaning username] sentenced to: share one specific number or account you''d normally keep private, as a starting point. Agree on one shared financial goal or account that''s fully visible to both, while leaving room for personal accounts elsewhere.','Bunny Court closed Financial Transparency Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-046','spectrum_4','BALANCED + BALANCED','You''re both fine with a mixed setup — some shared, some separate, no strong feelings either way.','This case barely needed a hearing. Just don''t let "we''re fine" mean you never actually check in on the shared parts.','[username A] sentenced to: review your shared expenses together once this month. [username B] sentenced to: come prepared with actual numbers, not vibes. Set one shared financial goal for this quarter — big or small — and check progress together.','Bunny Court closed Financial Transparency Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-046','spectrum_5','BALANCED + PRIVACY','One of you is fine with mixed visibility. The other genuinely values keeping their finances more private.','Low-conflict gap — the balanced partner has the flexibility to respect that boundary without it costing much.','[Balanced username] sentenced to: don''t push for more access than what''s already been offered. [Privacy-leaning username] sentenced to: share the basics of the shared expenses, even if the rest stays separate. Agree on which shared costs get full visibility, and let the rest stay private without guilt.','Bunny Court closed Financial Transparency Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-046','spectrum_6','PRIVACY + PRIVACY','Neither of you is eager to fully merge finances — you both value keeping some financial independence.','No conflict here, plenty of couples run this way successfully. Just make sure "separate" doesn''t quietly become "we never talk about money at all."','[username A] sentenced to: share one honest number about your finances this month, even if it''s uncomfortable. [username B] sentenced to: same — your turn. Once a quarter, do a no-pressure "state of our finances" conversation — just the big picture, nothing forced.','Bunny Court closed Financial Transparency Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-062','spectrum_1','SAVER + SAVER','You''re both wired to save first, spend later — impulse purchases are rare in this household.','Low financial friction here. The only note: make sure saving everything doesn''t mean never enjoying any of it.','[username A] sentenced to: make one small, guilt-free purchase this week, just because. [username B] sentenced to: same — no over-analyzing it. Set aside a small "no questions asked" fun fund you both pull from without discussion.','Bunny Court closed Different Spending Habits Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-062','spectrum_2','SAVER + BALANCED','One of you saves by instinct. The other spends more freely but isn''t reckless about it.','Not really a conflict — the balanced partner has room to lean toward saving without much sacrifice.','[Saver username] sentenced to: agree to one planned purchase without a week of research first. [Balanced username] sentenced to: run bigger purchases by your partner before pulling the trigger. Set one shared savings goal with a fun reward attached at the end.','Bunny Court closed Different Spending Habits Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-062','spectrum_3','SAVER + SPENDER','One of you saves every extra dollar on instinct. The other sees money as something to enjoy now, not hoard for later.','Neither instinct is wrong — one of you finds security in saving, the other finds joy in spending. The tension isn''t really about money, it''s about what feels safe versus what feels alive.','[Saver username] sentenced to: greenlight one spontaneous purchase this month without a research phase. [Spender username] sentenced to: run purchases over a set amount by your partner first, no exceptions. Agree on one joint "fun budget" each month that''s fully guilt-free to spend, no negotiation needed after it''s set.','Bunny Court closed Different Spending Habits Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-062','spectrum_4','BALANCED + BALANCED','Neither of you leans hard toward saving or spending — you''re both reasonably moderate about money.','This case barely needed a hearing. Just make sure "we''re fine" doesn''t mean nobody''s actually tracking the shared budget.','[username A] sentenced to: check the shared budget once this month, actual numbers. [username B] sentenced to: same — compare notes together. Set one small shared financial goal for fun — a trip, a gadget, whatever — and track it together.','Bunny Court closed Different Spending Habits Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-062','spectrum_5','BALANCED + SPENDER','One of you is moderate about money. The other leans toward spending more freely.','Manageable gap — the balanced partner has flexibility to enjoy a bit more spontaneous spending without much cost.','[Balanced username] sentenced to: greenlight one spontaneous purchase this month without much pushback. [Spender username] sentenced to: check in before any purchase over an agreed amount. Set a monthly "fun money" cap you''re both comfortable with, no judgment within that limit.','Bunny Court closed Different Spending Habits Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-062','spectrum_6','SPENDER + SPENDER','You both lean toward enjoying money now rather than stashing it away — spontaneous purchases are a shared love language here.','No conflict between you, but the court notes future-you might appreciate a little more saved.','[username A] sentenced to: set aside one small automatic savings amount this month, no debate. [username B] sentenced to: same — match the amount. Set one shared savings goal with a tangible reward at the end — something worth delaying gratification for, together.','Bunny Court closed Different Spending Habits Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-053','spectrum_1','ANXIOUS + ANXIOUS','You both notice distance quickly and want reassurance fast when something feels off between you.','You''re matched here, which means neither of you is left wondering alone — but it also means uncertainty can escalate quickly for both of you at once.','[username A] sentenced to: name the specific reassurance you need, instead of just reaching out more. [username B] sentenced to: same. Agree on a simple check-in phrase you can both use when anxiety spikes, so it doesn''t have to build in silence.','Bunny Court closed Anxious vs Avoidant Attachment Trial.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-053','spectrum_2','ANXIOUS + BALANCED/SECURE','One of you notices distance quickly and wants reassurance. The other feels pretty steady either way.','The balanced partner has room here to offer reassurance without it costing them much security of their own.','[Anxious-leaning username] sentenced to: name what reassurance would actually help, instead of assuming they should know. [Balanced/Secure username] sentenced to: offer it proactively sometimes, before it''s asked for. Set one small, regular check-in ritual — even a daily one-line text — that runs on its own, without either of you having to ask.','Bunny Court closed Anxious vs Avoidant Attachment Trial.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-053','spectrum_3','ANXIOUS + AVOIDANT','One of you needs closeness and reassurance to feel secure. The other needs space and independence to feel secure.','Neither of you is doing this on purpose — one of you feels safest when things are close and confirmed, the other feels safest with room to breathe. The push-pull isn''t a flaw in either of you, it''s two different definitions of safety meeting in the same relationship.','[Anxious-leaning username] sentenced to: ask for reassurance directly instead of testing for it. [Avoidant-leaning username] sentenced to: give a small, direct reassurance even when instinct says to pull back. Agree on what "space" and "closeness" actually look like for each of you, in specific terms — not just the words themselves.','Bunny Court closed Anxious vs Avoidant Attachment Trial.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-053','spectrum_4','BALANCED/SECURE + BALANCED/SECURE','Neither of you swings hard toward needing constant reassurance or needing a lot of space — you''re both fairly steady.','This case barely needed a hearing. Steady is a good place to be.','[username A] sentenced to: name one small thing you''d still like more of, even if things feel fine. [username B] sentenced to: take it seriously, not dismiss it as unnecessary. Once a month, ask each other: "is there anything you need more of from me right now?"','Bunny Court closed Anxious vs Avoidant Attachment Trial.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-053','spectrum_5','BALANCED/SECURE + AVOIDANT','One of you is fairly steady. The other genuinely needs more independence and space to feel secure.','Low-friction gap — the balanced partner has room to give that space without feeling shut out.','[Balanced/Secure username] sentenced to: don''t read space as distance-with-a-meaning. [Avoidant-leaning username] sentenced to: give a small heads-up before pulling back, so it doesn''t read as withdrawal. Agree on a signal for "I need some space, not upset with you" so it never has to be guessed at.','Bunny Court closed Anxious vs Avoidant Attachment Trial.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-053','spectrum_6','AVOIDANT + AVOIDANT','You both feel most secure with independence and room to breathe — closeness doesn''t need to be constant to feel real.','No conflict here, this rhythm works fine for plenty of couples.','[username A] sentenced to: check in intentionally once this week, even briefly. [username B] sentenced to: same. Plan one small moment of closeness this week that doesn''t have to lead to anything else — just presence, no pressure.','Bunny Court closed Anxious vs Avoidant Attachment Trial.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-057','spectrum_1','OPEN + OPEN','You''re both genuinely comfortable with opposite-sex friendships — trust isn''t in question here.','No conflict, this is about as low-drama as this topic gets.','[username A] sentenced to: keep including your partner in that part of your life — introductions, updates, whatever feels natural. [username B] sentenced to: same. Plan a hangout that includes one of those friendships, so it''s part of your shared world, not separate from it.','Bunny Court closed The Opposite-Sex Friend Boundary Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-057','spectrum_2','OPEN + BALANCED','One of you is fully comfortable with opposite-sex friendships. The other''s fine too, just wants some context depending on the situation.','Not really a conflict — a little context usually settles this quickly.','[Open-leaning username] sentenced to: share a bit more context when it''s relevant, even if it feels unnecessary. [Balanced username] sentenced to: ask directly instead of quietly wondering. Introduce your partner to that friend if you haven''t already — removes most of the mystery.','Bunny Court closed The Opposite-Sex Friend Boundary Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-057','spectrum_3','OPEN + BOUNDARY','One of you sees opposite-sex friendships as no different from any other friendship. The other feels more comfortable with some boundaries around them.','Neither view is a trust issue by default — one of you separates friendship from romantic risk easily, the other feels more secure with clearer lines.','[Open-leaning username] sentenced to: hear out what specifically would help your partner feel secure, without dismissing it as controlling. [Boundary-leaning username] sentenced to: get specific about what boundary would actually help, rather than a general uneasy feeling. Talk through one specific friendship together and agree on what feels okay for both of you, rather than a blanket rule.','Bunny Court closed The Opposite-Sex Friend Boundary Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-057','spectrum_4','BALANCED + BALANCED','Neither of you has a strong stance here — it depends on the friendship and the context, case by case.','No conflict, reasonable people all around.','[username A] sentenced to: mention it if a specific friendship starts to feel like it needs a conversation. [username B] sentenced to: take that seriously without getting defensive. Talk through your general comfort zones once, just so you''re not guessing case by case.','Bunny Court closed The Opposite-Sex Friend Boundary Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-057','spectrum_5','BALANCED + BOUNDARY','One of you doesn''t have a strong preference. The other genuinely feels more comfortable with clearer boundaries.','Low-friction gap — the balanced partner can accommodate that boundary without much cost.','[Balanced username] sentenced to: respect the boundary without needing it fully justified. [Boundary-leaning username] sentenced to: name the boundary specifically, rather than a vague discomfort. Write down one or two specific agreements about opposite-sex friendships that you''re both comfortable with.','Bunny Court closed The Opposite-Sex Friend Boundary Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-057','spectrum_6','BOUNDARY + BOUNDARY','You both feel more comfortable with clear boundaries around opposite-sex friendships — this isn''t really a point of tension between you.','No conflict here, you''re aligned on where the lines sit.','[username A] sentenced to: revisit the agreed boundaries occasionally, since comfort levels can shift. [username B] sentenced to: same. Check in once a year on whether your boundaries still feel right, rather than assuming they''re set in stone.','Bunny Court closed The Opposite-Sex Friend Boundary Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-058','spectrum_1','VERBAL + VERBAL','You both think out loud — conversations happen in real time, mid-thought and all.','No mismatch here, you process the same way, which usually means fewer things go unsaid.','[username A] sentenced to: let the other finish a thought before jumping in. [username B] sentenced to: same. Talk through something small out loud together this week, no filtering, just to enjoy how naturally that works for you two.','Bunny Court closed Different Communication Styles Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-058','spectrum_2','VERBAL + BALANCED','One of you processes out loud immediately. The other needs a little time first, but not a lot.','Not a real conflict — the balanced partner can meet the verbal processor partway without much strain.','[Verbal-processor username] sentenced to: give a beat before expecting an immediate response. [Balanced username] sentenced to: jump in a little sooner than instinct says to. Pick a small, low-stakes topic this week and talk it through together, out loud, start to finish.','Bunny Court closed Different Communication Styles Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-058','spectrum_3','VERBAL + INTERNAL','One of you needs to talk it out immediately to process. The other needs quiet time alone before they can even find the words.','Neither style is wrong — one of you thinks by talking, the other thinks by going quiet first.','[Verbal-processor username] sentenced to: ask how much time is needed, instead of assuming "now" works. [Internal-processor username] sentenced to: give a rough timeframe — "give me 20 minutes" — instead of just going quiet. Agree on a simple signal for "I need to think first" that doesn''t require explaining it every time.','Bunny Court closed Different Communication Styles Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-058','spectrum_4','BALANCED + BALANCED','Neither of you leans hard toward talking immediately or needing a lot of processing time — you both flex depending on the topic.','No conflict here, this case barely needed a hearing.','[username A] sentenced to: name one thing that''s been sitting unsaid, even if small. [username B] sentenced to: actually make time to hear it out. Once a month, ask each other if there''s anything that''s gone unsaid lately.','Bunny Court closed Different Communication Styles Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-058','spectrum_5','BALANCED + INTERNAL','One of you can go either way. The other genuinely needs processing time before talking things through.','Low-friction gap — the balanced partner can wait it out without much frustration.','[Balanced username] sentenced to: don''t push for an immediate conversation. [Internal-processor username] sentenced to: give a specific time to circle back, rather than leaving it open-ended. Practice the "give me some time, then we''ll talk" hand-off once this week, on something low-stakes.','Bunny Court closed Different Communication Styles Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-058','spectrum_6','INTERNAL + INTERNAL','You both need time alone to process before talking things through — conversations don''t happen in the heat of the moment for either of you.','No conflict here, just make sure "I need time" doesn''t quietly turn into "we never actually circle back."','[username A] sentenced to: set an actual time to revisit the conversation, not just "later." [username B] sentenced to: hold each other to that timeframe. Agree on a standard "processing window" — say, a day — so neither of you has to guess how long is too long.','Bunny Court closed Different Communication Styles Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-059','spectrum_1','OPEN + OPEN','You''re both fairly open about processing relationship stuff publicly or with your circles — venting isn''t taboo for either of you.','You''re aligned here, which means neither of you is caught off guard by the other''s need to process out loud.','[username A] sentenced to: give a heads-up before posting anything that''s clearly about a specific moment. [username B] sentenced to: same. Agree on what''s fair game to share and what stays just between you, so it''s a shared line, not a guess.','Bunny Court closed The Overshare on Social Media Case.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-059','spectrum_2','OPEN + BALANCED','One of you processes things more publicly. The other''s more selective, sharing the big stuff with close friends only.','Not really a conflict — a little more discretion usually closes this gap easily.','[Open-sharing username] sentenced to: check in before venting about something specific to the relationship. [Balanced username] sentenced to: share what you''re comfortable with, rather than defaulting to total privacy. Agree on a general rule for what''s okay to share and with whom, so it doesn''t have to be renegotiated every time.','Bunny Court closed The Overshare on Social Media Case.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-059','spectrum_3','OPEN + PRIVATE','One of you processes relationship stuff by talking about it, sometimes publicly. The other feels strongly that relationship matters should stay between the two of you.','Neither instinct is wrong — one of you finds relief in external processing, the other finds safety in privacy.','[Open-sharing username] sentenced to: check in before sharing anything that''s identifiably about your partner or the relationship. [Private-leaning username] sentenced to: offer one acceptable outlet — a close friend, a journal — so the need to process doesn''t disappear entirely. Agree explicitly on what counts as "private" for you two, so it''s not assumed differently by each of you.','Bunny Court closed The Overshare on Social Media Case.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-059','spectrum_4','BALANCED + BALANCED','Neither of you leans hard toward oversharing or total privacy — you''re both fairly selective about what goes public.','No conflict here, this case barely needed a hearing.','[username A] sentenced to: check in if something feels like it''s crossed a line. [username B] sentenced to: take that seriously without getting defensive. Talk through your general boundaries once, just so you''re both working from the same understanding.','Bunny Court closed The Overshare on Social Media Case.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-059','spectrum_5','BALANCED + PRIVATE','One of you doesn''t have strong feelings either way. The other genuinely values keeping relationship matters private.','Low-friction gap — the balanced partner can lean toward privacy without much cost.','[Balanced username] sentenced to: default to discretion when in doubt. [Private-leaning username] sentenced to: name specifically what feels off-limits, so it''s clear rather than assumed. Agree on a simple rule — "if it''s about a disagreement, it stays between us" — and stick to it.','Bunny Court closed The Overshare on Social Media Case.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-059','spectrum_6','PRIVATE + PRIVATE','You both keep relationship matters close to the chest — this has never really been a point of tension between you.','No conflict here, you''re aligned on keeping things private.','[username A] sentenced to: make sure "private" doesn''t mean "we never process it with anyone at all." [username B] sentenced to: same. Identify one trusted person each of you can talk to when needed, so privacy doesn''t turn into isolation.','Bunny Court closed The Overshare on Social Media Case.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-061','spectrum_1','HIGHER + HIGHER','You''re both wanting more frequency than what''s currently happening — the gap here isn''t between you two, it''s between where you are and where you''d both like to be.','Genuinely aligned desire, which makes this more of a scheduling and energy conversation than a mismatch conversation.','[username A] sentenced to: name one specific barrier that''s been getting in the way lately (time, stress, energy). [username B] sentenced to: same. Set aside intentional time this week, not just hoping it happens spontaneously.','Bunny Court closed The Mismatched Libido Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-061','spectrum_2','HIGHER + BALANCED','One of you wants more frequency. The other''s content with things as they are, but not opposed to more.','Not really a mismatch — the balanced partner has room to meet the other partway without it feeling like an obligation.','[Higher-desire username] sentenced to: express interest without it feeling like pressure. [Balanced username] sentenced to: initiate occasionally, rather than always waiting to be asked. Plan one intentional evening this week, no expectations beyond just prioritizing each other.','Bunny Court closed The Mismatched Libido Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-061','spectrum_3','HIGHER + LOWER','One of you wants this to happen more often. The other is genuinely content with less right now.','This is a real difference in needs, not a reflection of how either of you feels about the relationship — desire runs on its own timeline for different people, often for reasons that have nothing to do with the relationship itself.','[Higher-desire username] sentenced to: express the need without framing it as a complaint. [Lower-desire username] sentenced to: share what''s actually going on for you lately, even if it''s hard to name. Talk about this outside of the moment itself — not right before or after — so it''s a conversation, not a negotiation.','Bunny Court closed The Mismatched Libido Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-061','spectrum_4','BALANCED + BALANCED','Neither of you has strong feelings about changing the current frequency — it feels roughly right to both of you.','No conflict here, this case barely needed a hearing.','[username A] sentenced to: check in occasionally, since needs can shift over time. [username B] sentenced to: same. Keep the door open for this conversation to come back up naturally, without it needing to be a big deal.','Bunny Court closed The Mismatched Libido Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-061','spectrum_5','BALANCED + LOWER','One of you is fine with the current frequency. The other genuinely feels it could be less right now.','Low-friction gap — the balanced partner has room to adjust expectations without it feeling like a loss.','[Balanced username] sentenced to: don''t take a lower-desire stretch personally. [Lower-desire username] sentenced to: share what''s contributing to it, if you''re able to name it. Focus on other forms of closeness this week — the kind that don''t come with expectations attached.','Bunny Court closed The Mismatched Libido Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-061','spectrum_6','LOWER + LOWER','You''re both feeling less inclined toward this right now — the frequency currently feels fine to both of you.','No conflict between you, this happens for plenty of couples during different seasons of life.','[username A] sentenced to: stay open to talking about it if that changes. [username B] sentenced to: same. Check in on this occasionally, without pressure, just to stay on the same page as things shift.','Bunny Court closed The Mismatched Libido Hearing.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-063','spectrum_1','PHYSICAL + PHYSICAL','You both feel closest through physical affection — touch does more for your connection than conversation does.','You''re aligned here, which means this need is being met on both sides without much translation required.','[username A] sentenced to: keep initiating physical affection, it''s clearly landing. [username B] sentenced to: same. Build in a small physical ritual — a hug at the door, a hand on the back — something consistent, not just occasional.','Bunny Court closed Different Definitions of Intimacy Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-063','spectrum_2','PHYSICAL + BALANCED','One of you feels closest through physical affection. The other gets it from a mix of touch and talking.','Not really a mismatch — the balanced partner has range to meet the physical-leaning partner without much effort.','[Physical-leaning username] sentenced to: name when physical affection specifically would help, rather than assuming it''s obvious. [Balanced username] sentenced to: lean into touch a bit more when that''s what''s needed. Spend one evening this week with more physical closeness than usual — no big gesture, just presence.','Bunny Court closed Different Definitions of Intimacy Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-063','spectrum_3','PHYSICAL + EMOTIONAL','One of you feels most connected through physical closeness. The other feels most connected through being deeply heard and understood.','Neither form of intimacy is more "real" than the other — one of you speaks through touch, the other through words. The disconnect happens when one offers their language and the other''s still waiting for theirs.','[Physical-leaning username] sentenced to: try leading with a real conversation once this week, before or instead of physical affection. [Emotional-leaning username] sentenced to: let a moment of physical closeness happen without needing it to lead to a conversation. Spend one evening doing both intentionally — a real conversation, followed by simple physical closeness — and see how the combination feels.','Bunny Court closed Different Definitions of Intimacy Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-063','spectrum_4','BALANCED + BALANCED','Neither of you leans hard toward one form of intimacy — you both get what you need from a mix of touch and conversation.','No conflict here, this case barely needed a hearing.','[username A] sentenced to: name which one you''ve been missing lately, if either. [username B] sentenced to: actually offer more of it. Once a month, ask each other which kind of closeness has felt more present lately, and which could use more attention.','Bunny Court closed Different Definitions of Intimacy Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-063','spectrum_5','BALANCED + EMOTIONAL','One of you gets what you need from a mix of both. The other feels most connected through deep conversation specifically.','Low-friction gap — the balanced partner has range to lean into conversation more without losing much.','[Balanced username] sentenced to: initiate a real conversation sometimes, not just physical affection. [Emotional-leaning username] sentenced to: name what "being heard" actually looks like in practice. Set aside one no-phones conversation this week, just to talk, no other agenda.','Bunny Court closed Different Definitions of Intimacy Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-063','spectrum_6','EMOTIONAL + EMOTIONAL','You both feel most connected through real conversation and feeling understood — physical affection matters, but it''s not the main language here.','No conflict here, you''re speaking the same intimacy language.','[username A] sentenced to: keep making time for real conversation, it''s clearly working. [username B] sentenced to: same. Have one deep, undistracted conversation this week — no phones, no time limit.','Bunny Court closed Different Definitions of Intimacy Court.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-041','spectrum_1','SPACE + SPACE','When things get tense, you both go quiet to process — neither of you is naturally the one pushing to talk it out right away.','You''re both wired the same way here, which usually means less pressure in the moment — but it also means disagreements can sit unresolved longer than either of you means them to.','[username A] sentenced to: after your quiet time, be the one to open the door back up — even just "ready to talk when you are." [username B] sentenced to: same — whoever''s ready first, speaks first, no waiting for the other to go first. Agree on a rough "we''ll check back in by ___" window, so quiet doesn''t accidentally turn into avoidance.','Bunny Court closed The Silent Treatment Case.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-041','spectrum_2','SPACE + BALANCED','One of you needs real quiet to process. The other can go either way — comfortable with space, but also fine talking sooner if needed.','The balanced partner has more room to adjust here without much cost — a little patience from them goes a long way for the one who needs the space.','[Space-leaning username] sentenced to: give a quick heads-up before going quiet — "I need some time" changes everything. [Balanced username] sentenced to: hold off on checking in until the agreed time, even if you''re ready sooner. Agree on one phrase that means "I need space" so it never has to be guessed at again.','Bunny Court closed The Silent Treatment Case.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-041','spectrum_3','SPACE + TALK（已确认案例）','When things get hard, one of you goes quiet to process. The other reads the silence as being shut out.','Neither of you is doing this to hurt the other — one of you needs space to think, the other needs reassurance that space isn''t the same as leaving. The real issue isn''t the silence itself, it''s that neither of you knows what it means in the moment.','[Space-leaning username] sentenced to: next time it happens, send one line — "I need a bit of time, not mad at you, back by tonight." [Talk-leaning username] sentenced to: agree not to double-text during that window. Before this happens again, sit down and agree on what silence will mean going forward — even a one-line house rule you both remember next time it gets tense.','Bunny Court closed The Silent Treatment Case.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-041','spectrum_4','BALANCED + BALANCED','Neither of you leans hard toward silence or immediate talk-it-out — you''re both fairly adaptable when things get tense.','This case barely needed to go to court. The only note: don''t let "we''re usually fine" mean an issue never actually gets fully talked through.','[username A] sentenced to: name one thing from the last disagreement that''s still unresolved, even a small one. [username B] sentenced to: actually sit down and hear it out, no matter how small it seems. Once a month, ask each other: "anything we brushed past that''s still bugging you?"','Bunny Court closed The Silent Treatment Case.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-041','spectrum_5','BALANCED + TALK','One of you can flex either way. The other really needs to talk it through in the moment — waiting it out doesn''t sit well with them.','The balanced partner has the flexibility to meet the other where they are — a little more patience for immediate conversation costs them less than it would the other way around.','[Balanced username] sentenced to: stay in the conversation a bit longer than instinct says to, even if you''d rather take space. [Talk-leaning username] sentenced to: name what you actually need to hear to feel okay, instead of just wanting "to talk." Next disagreement, try talking it through for 10 minutes before either of you decides you need space — see how far it gets.','Bunny Court closed The Silent Treatment Case.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('CASE-041','spectrum_6','TALK + TALK','Neither of you does the quiet thing — when something''s wrong, you both want it out in the open, fast.','No mismatch in style here — the risk isn''t silence, it''s talking before either of you has actually cooled down.','[username A] sentenced to: take 10 minutes to breathe before diving into the next hard conversation. [username B] sentenced to: same — race to talk it out less, race to calm down first more. Agree on a "cool-down first, talk second" rule for the next disagreement — even 10 minutes counts.','Bunny Court closed The Silent Treatment Case.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-01','low_stakes_timing_gap','LOW-STAKES TIMING GAP','A message waited longer than one person expected, but neither testimony points to a larger pattern.','This looks like timing, not a hidden statement about the relationship. A tiny status signal can prevent unnecessary lore.','The slower replier sends a brief status when practical. The waiting partner allows ordinary delay without opening a second case in their imagination. Choose one phrase that means “I saw this and will return.”','Read receipts remain emotionally unqualified evidence.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-01','silence_means_different_things','SILENCE MEANS DIFFERENT THINGS','One person experiences silence as neutral processing. The other experiences the same gap as uncertainty or disconnection.','Neither meaning is automatic. The relationship needs a shared translation for busy, space, and unresolved conflict.','The person going quiet names which state they are in and gives a realistic return point. The other respects that window and saves additional messages for new information. Define three signals: busy, need space, ready to talk.','Silence now requires captions.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-01','avoidance_loop','AVOIDANCE LOOP','Important messages repeatedly disappear into long gaps, while follow-ups become more urgent and the original issue remains untouched.','Space can support a conversation; indefinite silence replaces it. The Court orders a pause with an end point, not instant resolution.','The withdrawing partner gives a return time they can keep. The pursuing partner asks once for confirmation and does not use the waiting window to expand the case. Use a repair script: “I need ___ time. I am not leaving. Let’s return at ___.”','Pause granted; disappearance denied. ---','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-02','history_not_a_current_issue','HISTORY, NOT A CURRENT ISSUE','An old connection remains visible, but the evidence does not show active interference in the current relationship.','A follow is not a confession. The Court finds history in the algorithm and no automatic threat in the present.','The person with the history answers reasonable factual questions once. The concerned partner receives the answer without turning the ex into a recurring background character. Name what current behavior makes trust feel strong today.','The algorithm has no relationship jurisdiction.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-02','context_gap','CONTEXT GAP','The contact itself may be ordinary, but one person has been filling missing context with increasingly elaborate possibilities.','Transparency can reduce mystery without creating surveillance. The useful facts are frequency, purpose, and whether anything is being hidden.','The partner with contact shares relevant context before it becomes a discovery. The other asks directly for the information they need rather than monitoring around the edges. Agree on which kinds of ex contact deserve a heads-up.','Context admitted; detective work retired.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-02','boundary_conversation_required','BOUNDARY CONVERSATION REQUIRED','At least one person experiences the current contact as secretive, emotionally central, or outside the relationship’s understood limits.','The Court will not make a universal ex rule. It will require a specific agreement based on behavior, privacy, and the commitments you actually made.','One partner states what contact they want to continue and why. The other states the precise boundary needed to remain comfortable, without demanding erasure of a whole past. Write one mutual rule covering private messages, emotional support, and disclosure.','The past may exist; the present gets a vote. ---','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-03','friendship_with_clear_edges','FRIENDSHIP WITH CLEAR EDGES','The friendship provides normal support and neither person reports secrecy, romantic framing, or displacement.','Closeness outside a couple is not automatically betrayal. Healthy relationships can contain other meaningful people with understandable boundaries.','The friend-connected partner keeps ordinary transparency. The concerned partner identifies one reassurance that supports trust without policing friendship. Name what makes an outside friendship feel included rather than hidden.','Friendship remains legal; secrecy remains under review.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-03','emotional_priority_drift','EMOTIONAL PRIORITY DRIFT','One outside connection has become the first destination for thoughts, comfort, or private processing that the relationship rarely receives.','This is less about banning a friend than noticing where intimacy is flowing. The couple needs a deliberate route back to each other.','The externally focused partner brings one meaningful topic back into the relationship before outsourcing it. The other creates space to receive it without immediate correction or interrogation. Schedule one weekly check-in for the kind of conversation that has been happening elsewhere.','Emotional traffic rerouted, not closed.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-03','trust_repair_required','TRUST REPAIR REQUIRED','Relevant contact, feelings, or context was hidden, minimized, or framed differently depending on who was asking.','The central issue is not the label “emotional cheating”; it is whether agreed trust was bypassed. Repair needs accurate disclosure and a boundary both people knowingly choose.','The person who concealed information provides the relevant truth without drip-feeding. The hurt partner states what is needed for repair and what cannot be promised away today. Agree on immediate boundaries and a later review point; avoid forcing instant forgiveness.','Honesty remains the least dramatic route forward. ---','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-04','privacy_values_align','PRIVACY VALUES ALIGN','Both people understand what is private, what is practically shared, and what openness means when it is freely chosen.','Trust does not require identical phone habits. Your rule works because both people recognize it as consent, not proof collection.','Each person restates one privacy boundary and one form of voluntary openness they are comfortable offering. Review practical access—emergency contacts, photos, navigation—without turning it into unrestricted inspection.','The phone is not a courtroom exhibit by default.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-04','reassurance_and_privacy_collide','REASSURANCE AND PRIVACY COLLIDE','One person believes access would settle uncertainty. The other experiences that access as loss of ordinary personal space.','Surveillance cannot create durable reassurance, and “privacy” cannot end every reasonable question. The need must be answered more directly.','The reassurance-seeking partner names the exact concern without requesting an unlimited search. The privacy-seeking partner answers that concern clearly and offers one voluntary, bounded reassurance. Write a phone rule covering permission, notifications, photos, and emergencies.','Trust may ask questions; it may not execute a warrant.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-04','trust_breach_on_either_side','TRUST BREACH ON EITHER SIDE','Privacy was crossed, important truth was concealed, or both happened in the same unhappy sequence.','One breach does not cancel the other. Repair requires separate ownership: what was hidden, what was searched, and what each damaged.','The person who snooped owns that choice without using discovery as retroactive permission. The person who concealed relevant information answers for that choice without using privacy to erase impact. Decide immediate boundaries and whether trust repair needs time or outside support.','Two issues separated; neither swept under the screen protector. ---','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-06','family_included_couple_centered','FAMILY INCLUDED, COUPLE CENTERED','Family is present without running the relationship’s board meetings. Both partners still experience the couple as the decision-making center.','The Court approves connection with healthy jurisdiction. Keep advice optional and plans confirmable.','Each partner thanks one helpful family contribution and names one area that remains the couple’s decision. Agree on what news is shared freely and what requires both people’s okay.','Family welcome; voting rights limited.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-06','boundary_needs_a_spokesperson','BOUNDARY NEEDS A SPOKESPERSON','The couple mostly agrees on the line, but the line becomes blurry when one family applies pressure in real time.','A boundary communicated only by the in-lawed partner can feel like personal rejection. The directly connected partner needs to carry the message.','The family-connected partner states the shared boundary using “we.” The other avoids becoming the visible enforcer unless direct disrespect occurs. Rehearse one sentence for invitations, advice, and private questions.','Diplomatic credentials issued to the correct household.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-06','couple_authority_is_being_bypassed','COUPLE AUTHORITY IS BEING BYPASSED','Private conflict, money, housing, parenting, or major plans are being shaped by family without both partners’ agreement.','Support can be valuable; outside authority over the couple is a different arrangement. The pair must decide what information and decisions leave the room.','The partner whose family is involved pauses new commitments and communicates the limit. The other states the impact and desired boundary without demanding family cutoff as the first move. Create a couple-first rule for major decisions and a plan for handling pushback.','Holidays remain advanced-level diplomacy. ---','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-07','same_destination_adjustable_route','SAME DESTINATION, ADJUSTABLE ROUTE','The horizon looks similar from both seats. The disagreements live mostly in dates, routes, and whose spreadsheet gets opened first.','You do not need a complete five-year contract. You need the next shared decision and permission to update the map.','Each names one commitment for the next twelve months and one assumption still open to change. Build a one-year plan with a review date instead of solving all five years tonight.','Future aligned; crystal ball returned.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-07','timing_or_route_mismatch','TIMING OR ROUTE MISMATCH','Both people recognize parts of the same future, but the order, location, career cost, or pace is not equally workable.','“Eventually” can conceal a real timing conflict. The Court orders dates, dependencies, and tradeoffs into the conversation without forcing an immediate promise.','The faster-moving partner names the latest timeline that remains honest for them. The slower or differently routed partner names what must happen before commitment becomes realistic. Compare two plausible timelines and identify the first decision point.','Route recalculation permitted; destination still under joint review.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-07','different_destination','DIFFERENT DESTINATION','A core future—children, marriage, location, lifestyle, or career structure—matters deeply to one person and is not desired by the other.','This is not a communication glitch to be solved with better phrasing. Care can be real while future requirements remain incompatible.','Each states the need without offering a hopeful maybe they cannot sustain. Each listens for the actual answer rather than negotiating with potential. Decide whether this needs time, a decision date, or support beyond the app; do not force a twenty-minute verdict.','No life plan has been sentenced to false agreement. ---','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-09','memory_mismatch','MEMORY MISMATCH','Both people remember the event differently, and neither account alone resolves what was said or intended.','Memory is imperfect; impact still happened. The Court does not need one person to surrender their reality in order to make a repair.','Each gives one concrete recollection and one impact statement without declaring the other dishonest. Each acknowledges what can be true in the other account. Agree on the fact you share, the detail you do not, and what should happen differently next time.','Certainty unavailable; respect still required.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-09','dismissal_pattern','DISMISSAL PATTERN','The conflict is no longer only about memory. One person repeatedly leaves the conversation feeling foolish, dramatic, or unsure they are allowed to trust their own experience.','Whether the label “gaslighting” fits is not the Court’s decision. Repeated dismissal is enough reason to change how this conversation happens.','The minimizing partner stops debating the legitimacy of the feeling and responds to one concrete impact. The affected partner records examples for their own clarity and chooses a boundary for conversations that become destabilizing. Use a written recap after one important conversation, only if both people can do so safely.','The label remains unruled; the impact is officially on the record.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-09','safety_coercion_concern','SAFETY/COERCION CONCERN','The answers suggest fear, control, retaliation, isolation, or a repeated campaign to undermine one person’s confidence in their own reality.','The Court is ending the bit. A couple’s quiz cannot safely adjudicate coercion or abuse, and joint confrontation may not be the right next move.','The person feeling unsafe prioritizes private support from someone trusted or an appropriate professional resource. The app does not instruct them to disclose plans, confront, or negotiate in a way that could increase risk. None required. Safety does not require a joint exercise.','Playful ruling withheld. Your safety matters more than the format. ---','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-10','work_connection_manageable_feeling','WORK CONNECTION, MANAGEABLE FEELING','The work relationship appears ordinary; the jealousy is real but not currently supported by concealed behavior.','Feelings may request reassurance without appointing themselves investigator. A small amount of context can settle this without policing a workplace.','The work-connected partner offers relevant context voluntarily. The jealous partner names the reassurance they need and avoids monitoring or attacking a third person. Agree on one easy check-in after work events.','The office group chat has no relationship jurisdiction.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-10','specific_boundary_needed','SPECIFIC BOUNDARY NEEDED','One identifiable behavior—not the coworker’s existence—is creating discomfort: private late-night messages, flirtatious tone, excluded social plans, or missing context.','Specific behavior can receive a specific agreement. Blanket restrictions and vague “just trust me” answers are both too blunt.','One partner describes the behavior and impact without demanding workplace isolation. The other proposes a boundary they can genuinely follow and a reassurance that remains voluntary. Write one work-social rule and one thing that remains normal professional freedom.','Boundary tailored; coworker not tried in absentia.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-10','trust_strain','TRUST STRAIN','Secrecy, flirting, changing stories, or repeated dismissal has made the coworker a symbol of a broader trust problem.','Removing one person from the contact list would not automatically repair the pattern. The relationship needs truth, ownership, and observable follow-through.','The partner whose behavior created uncertainty answers relevant questions without drip-feeding. The other states the condition needed for continued trust and allows time to verify change. Set one boundary, one transparency practice, and a review date.','The symbol has been separated from the actual issue. ---','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-11','ready_with_open_eyes','READY WITH OPEN EYES','Both people want the move and can discuss money, mess, privacy, and logistics without replacing details with “it’ll work out.”','The Court finds informed enthusiasm. Romance may pack the first box; a written household plan should pack the second.','Each owns one pre-move task and discloses one habit the other should not discover beside an unpacked sink. Complete a money, chores, guests, alone-time, and exit-plan checklist.','Lease discussion approved; impulse signature not required.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-11','same_goal_different_runway','SAME GOAL, DIFFERENT RUNWAY','Both people can imagine sharing a home, but one needs more savings, information, time, or evidence about daily compatibility.','“Not yet” can be a path rather than rejection when it contains real conditions and a review date.','The more ready partner stops converting excitement into urgency. The less ready partner names measurable conditions and a realistic time to revisit them. Run a short trial—extended stay, shared budget exercise, or chore week—without treating it as automatic consent.','Runway extended; destination still possible.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-11','pressure_is_driving_the_move','PRESSURE IS DRIVING THE MOVE','Money, housing urgency, fear, or relationship momentum is pushing faster than one person’s actual readiness.','Moving in is not a reassurance test. Consent to a shared home must survive the possibility of saying “not now.”','The hesitant partner states the no or not-yet clearly. The other helps separate the housing problem from the relationship decision and does not punish the boundary. List alternative housing or timing options before discussing commitment again.','Nobody is sentenced to sign a lease tonight. ---','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-13','different_work_mutually_recognized','DIFFERENT WORK, MUTUALLY RECOGNIZED','The contributions are not identical, but both people can name what the other carries and why the current season looks this way.','Fairness is not symmetry when capacity differs. Recognition plus consent keeps a temporary imbalance from becoming silent debt.','Each names one contribution they rely on from the other. Each also identifies one load that should be reviewed if the season continues. Schedule a brief capacity check two weeks from now.','The Court cannot count love, but it can notice labor.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-13','one_load_is_too_heavy','ONE LOAD IS TOO HEAVY','Both testimonies point to one person carrying more practical or emotional responsibility than they can comfortably sustain.','Good reasons may explain the imbalance; they do not make the overloaded person infinitely expandable. One concrete load must move.','The overloaded partner chooses the task or responsibility with highest relief value. The other owns it fully or helps secure outside support, including the noticing and follow-through. Transfer one complete responsibility for seven days and review the difference.','Capacity limit recognized before system failure.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-13','two_invisible_ledgers','TWO INVISIBLE LEDGERS','Both people feel they give more. Each ledger contains real entries, but the categories and exchange rates do not match.','This is not solved by proving whose list is longer. The Court orders shared definitions, priority loads, and fewer unspoken trades.','Each lists three recurring contributions and circles the one they most need recognized. Then each chooses one item from the other list to share or reduce. Build a one-week responsibility map without assigning relationship points.','Competing ledgers merged; winner column deleted. ---','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-14','difference_with_respect','DIFFERENCE WITH RESPECT','You disagree without turning the other person into a caricature. Curiosity remains present and nobody’s humanity is treated as debate material.','The Court allows political difference where respect and shared life remain intact. Agreement is not the only evidence of listening.','Each summarizes the other’s view in terms they accept before offering a response. Each names one shared value underneath the disagreement. Discuss one issue with a ten-minute limit and a mutual stop signal.','Democracy survives another dinner.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-14','debate_is_crowding_connection','DEBATE IS CROWDING CONNECTION','The topic returns often, tones sharpen, and the relationship begins hosting a panel discussion nobody requested.','A boundary around frequency and conduct is not avoidance. It is protection against turning every shared moment into a referendum.','The frequent initiator asks consent before reopening the topic. The other names what discussion format remains possible instead of using silence as the only boundary. Agree on no-insult rules, time limits, and topics reserved for deliberate conversations.','Campaign season officially shortened.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-14','core_value_incompatibility','CORE-VALUE INCOMPATIBILITY','The disagreement affects dignity, rights, safety, family plans, or the respect one person can feel inside the relationship.','This cannot be reduced to “agree to disagree.” The Court cannot decide compatibility, but it can refuse to disguise a core-value conflict as casual politics.','Each states the value and real-life consequence at stake. Each answers whether the relationship can honor that value without expecting the other to become someone else. Decide whether this needs more time, a decision, or qualified outside support; do not force a playful compromise.','The issue remains serious; the conversation is now accurately named. ---','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-15','mutual_high_volume_romance','MUTUAL HIGH-VOLUME ROMANCE','The affection is large, the enthusiasm is mutual, and “not tonight” remains a complete sentence.','Big gestures are not automatically suspicious. Consistency, consent, and room for ordinary days are the stronger evidence.','Each names one grand gesture they enjoy and one low-key behavior that builds trust over time. Plan one intentionally ordinary date and see whether the connection still feels good without fireworks.','Flowers admitted; consistency promoted to lead counsel.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-15','pace_mismatch','PACE MISMATCH','One person is enjoying the speed. The other likes the connection but needs more time, predictability, or room to choose each next step.','Slowing down is not rejection, and enthusiasm is not automatically manipulation. The test is whether the boundary is respected without persuasion becoming pressure.','The faster-moving partner asks before escalating plans or promises. The slower partner names a pace and form of affection that still feels welcome. Agree on one next step and pause larger promises until a set check-in.','Romance remains; fast-forward disabled.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-15','pressure_control_concern','PRESSURE/CONTROL CONCERN','Affection is paired with guilt, punishment, boundary-pushing, rapid dependence, isolation, or fear about saying no.','The Court is ending the romantic bit. Grand gestures never purchase access, commitment, forgiveness, or control.','The person feeling pressured prioritizes private support and their own safety. The app does not direct joint confrontation where doing so could increase risk. None required. Consent and safety do not need a couple exercise.','Playful ruling withheld. A “no” requires no romantic appeal. ---','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-16','busy_but_mutually_engaged','BUSY BUT MUTUALLY ENGAGED','Life is crowded, but both people initiate, explain changes, and eventually do what they said they would do.','Infrequency is not breadcrumbing when mutuality and follow-through remain visible. Protect the connection with realistic plans rather than guilt.','The busier partner offers one plan they can actually keep. The other accepts the real pace instead of comparing it with an imaginary ideal. Put one low-pressure plan on the calendar with a clear reschedule rule.','Busy schedule, full sentence.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-16','inconsistent_availability','INCONSISTENT AVAILABILITY','Interest appears, disappears, and returns with enough warmth to restart the question. Neither person has clearly named what they can sustain.','The Court cannot infer intent from message frequency. It can require availability and expectations to be stated without decorative ambiguity.','The inconsistent partner says what contact and commitment they can reliably offer. The waiting partner names the minimum pattern they are willing to participate in. Agree on one plan and one communication standard for the next two weeks, then review the evidence.','Tea leaves replaced with calendar entries.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-16','indefinite_waiting_room','INDEFINITE WAITING ROOM','Plans remain vague, follow-through is rare, and attention returns mainly when one person begins stepping away.','Whatever the intent, the current offer is intermittent. The receiving person is allowed to decide based on the pattern, not the potential occasionally advertised.','The inconsistent partner stops making promises they cannot schedule. The waiting partner chooses a boundary—specific plan by a date, a different relationship structure, or disengagement. Compare the actual offer with the minimum each person needs; do not negotiate with future versions.','Crumbs are not a meal plan. ---','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-18','normal_season_shift','NORMAL SEASON SHIFT','Closeness changed alongside stress, health, routine, or life season, and both people can recognize the context without assigning failure.','Relationships have changing rhythms. The Court finds no requirement to restore a previous frequency before understanding the present one.','Each names one form of non-demand affection that feels good now. Each respects that affection as complete, not a promise of escalation. Choose one pressure-free closeness ritual for the week.','A season is not a sentence.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-18','different_needs_shared_care','DIFFERENT NEEDS, SHARED CARE','One person misses physical connection more intensely. The other may need different timing, conditions, or kinds of closeness.','Desire cannot be argued into equality. A useful conversation protects both longing and consent without making either person defective.','The higher-desire partner speaks about feeling and connection without turning it into a quota. The lower-desire partner names what is welcome, what is not, and whether any context needs support. Make three lists privately, then compare: welcome, maybe/ask, and not now.','Closeness clarified; entitlement denied.','Calm, validating, low-blame.'),
('MIDDLE-18','pressure_safety_concern','PRESSURE/SAFETY CONCERN','The answers suggest obligation, fear, punishment, ignored refusal, pain, or pressure around intimacy.','The Court is ending the playful format. Consent must be freely given each time and can change at any point; relationship status does not replace it.','The person feeling pressured prioritizes safety, medical care where relevant, and confidential support. The app does not direct a joint exercise or disclose their raw input to the partner. None required. Safety and consent come before couple performance.','No one owes a performance. ---','Calm, validating, low-blame.');
